//****************************************************************************
// $Module type: Class Package
// $Module name: cFTThemeHost_mixin
// $Author     : Nils Svedmyr, RDC Tools International, <mailto:support@rdctools.com>
// Web-site    : http://www.rdctools.com
// Created     : 2026-08-13 (as the include file FTThemeHost.inc; a Mixin package since 2026-09-21)
//
// Description : Theme members shared by cFTLocalWebControlHost and cFTDbLocalWebControlHost.
//
//               The two FT host classes need identical behaviour but sit on different framework bases
//               (cLocalWebControlHost and cDbLocalWebControlHost, the latter reaching the same mixin via
//               cBaseDbLocalWebControlHost), so neither can inherit from the other. This is the framework's
//               own answer to that shape - a Mixin, imported with Import_Class_Protocol - and replaces an
//               #Include inside the class bodies: the Studio's parser has to resolve a class before the
//               Windows Designer will model an object of it, and a mixin is a construct it knows.
//
//               Not for Use on its own: each host Uses its framework base FIRST, and that is what declares
//               the host members set here (peThemePreference, psTheme, pbInitWebApp) and the tp* values.
//
//               Each host's own End_Construct_Object forwards first (the base sends
//               End_Construct_cWebHostAPI_mixin, which is what brings peThemePreference into existence)
//               and then sends ApplyFTTheme.
//
// WHAT THIS IS FOR
//               The FlexTron host carries the framework's theme switch: peThemePreference, from
//               cWebHostAPI_mixin.pkg. Setting it makes the framework put df-Dark or df-Light on the host
//               document's body, which is the document our cFTWebHtmlBox markup is mounted in - so one
//               property re-themes both the DataFlex web controls and, through palette.css, our own tokens.
//               peThemePreference is DF26-only; the build has been DF26-only since 2026-09-16, so the
//               former #IF (!@ > 250) gate around it is gone.
//
// This code is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY;
// without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.
//****************************************************************************
Use FTConstants.h.pkg           // CI_FTTheme*, giFTThemeMode, where the theme is saved
Use FTThemeColors.pkg           // FTWebBg, FTColorHex - the WebView2 back colour, and the page body's
Use FTMarkupBuilders.pkg        // StyleSheetLink, ScriptLink - the links the page's head and its includes are made of
Use FTAppGlyphs.pkg             // FTAppGlyphNames, FTAppGlyphStamp - the link to the application's own glyphs

Class cFTThemeHost_mixin is a Mixin

    // Seed giFTThemeMode from the saved setting, once per run. Deliberately lazy rather than done at
    // startup: OnStartup runs AFTER every object exists (see ApplyDarkStatusPanel at the foot of
    // DFRefactorFT.src), so the view hosts would already have been constructed and themed by then.
    // Whoever constructs first pays for one registry read; the other 32 hosts find the global set.
    Procedure SeedFTThemeMode
        Integer iMode
        If (giFTThemeMode = CI_FTThemeUnset) Begin
            // No application object yet - a dialog Used above it: there is nothing to read the choice through.
            If (ghoApplication = 0) Begin
                Procedure_Return
            End
            Get ReadInteger of ghoApplication CS_FTThemeSection CS_FTTheme CI_FTThemeDark to iMode
            // A value written by a future version - or a hand-edited registry - must not leave the
            // global on a meaningless number, so anything unrecognised falls back to dark.
            If (iMode < CI_FTThemeDark or iMode > CI_FTThemeSystem) Begin
                Move CI_FTThemeDark to iMode
            End
            Move iMode to giFTThemeMode
            // And every FT object built before now - waiting for the theme - takes it (FTThemeColors.pkg).
            Send FTThemeLateObjects
        End
    End_Procedure

    // Push giFTThemeMode onto this host. Called as the host is constructed, and again by a runtime
    // theme switch - broadcast it to re-read the global rather than setting peThemePreference directly,
    // so the mapping lives in exactly one place.
    Procedure ApplyFTTheme
        Integer eWanted
        Send SeedFTThemeMode
        // Built before the theme is known - a dialog Used above the application object: this host waits for
        // it (FTThemeLater) and applies nothing now. A guess would stick: ApplyWebView2DefaultBackground below
        // is once-only.
        If (giFTThemeMode = CI_FTThemeUnset) Begin
            Send FTThemeLater Self
            Procedure_Return
        End
        Move tpDarkMode to eWanted
        If (giFTThemeMode = CI_FTThemeLight) Begin
            Move tpLightMode to eWanted
        End
        If (giFTThemeMode = CI_FTThemeSystem) Begin
            Move tpSystemPreference to eWanted
        End
        Set peThemePreference to eWanted
        /* THE WHITE FLASH AT RESIZE (Nils 2026-08-15: "an annoying section ... only visible as a
           white horizontal line when resizing", seen on the Browse packages AND Libraries dialogs).

           Not a section - the WebView2 CONTROL's own background, which defaults to white and shows
           at the freshly exposed edge for the moment before the page repaints. cWebView2Browser
           (the framework base under every local host) exposes it as piBackColor -> ComBackColor;
           nothing ever set it, so every resize of every FT dialog flashed white on a dark theme.

           Set here, in the one place every FT host passes through, with the same colour the native
           side already uses - so a theme re-apply updates it too. Version-free: piBackColor exists
           on DF25's cWebView2Browser as well (read there, 2026-08-15). */
        Set piBackColor to (FTWebBg())
        /* AND THE NATIVE SURROUND. Color is piBackColor's twin on the DataFlex side - the panel
           margin around the browser control. 33 of the 42 FT hosts set it to exactly this and not
           one set anything else, so it was never a per-object decision; the nine that said nothing
           were simply left on the framework's pale default behind a dark page.

           HERE rather than in either class's Construct_Object, for two reasons: this include is what
           the two host classes SHARE (cFTDbLocalWebControlHost cannot extend cFTLocalWebControlHost -
           see its header), and this procedure is what a runtime theme switch re-broadcasts, so the
           surround follows a switch instead of being frozen at construction.

           The cost, stated plainly: End_Construct_Object runs after the object body, so this now WINS
           over a per-instance Color. Nothing in the app sets a different one - if something ever needs
           to, it belongs after this, not before. */
        Set Color to (FTWebBg())
        // And the COMPOSITOR's background - the third painter, process-wide, once. See
        // ApplyWebView2DefaultBackground in FTThemeColors.pkg for the whole story.
        Send ApplyWebView2DefaultBackground
    End_Procedure

    /* THE HOST SAYS WHAT IT IS: FtHost (0.9.21). The page of an FT host carries the class FtHost in two places.
       The host's own element has it - psCSSClass, set here, and named by the host classes' tag for the Studio's
       Designer, which runs none of this code - and so has the page's body (VirtualHtml below): the framework
       hangs a date picker's panel and its balloons on the body, outside the host's element.

       ftcontrols.css keys the FT look of the framework's OWN controls on it. A cWebForm, a cWebEdit or a
       cWebCombo inside an FT host reads like its cFT twin with no class of its own, so a control needs a cFT
       class for what it does, not for how it looks.

       psCSSClass REPLACES the class list: a host that sets its own keeps FtHost in front of it. Sent by each
       host's End_Construct_Object, after the object's body. */
    Procedure ApplyFTHostClass
        String sClasses
        Move (Trim(psCSSClass(Self))) to sClasses
        If (Pos(" FtHost ", (" " + sClasses + " ")) = 0) ;
            Set psCSSClass to (Trim("FtHost " + sClasses))
    End_Procedure

    /* THE PAGE BODY IS THE WHITE (2026-08-15, probe 2). With the wrapper control dark (probe 1
       turned it red - piBackColor works) and the compositor painted RED by the env var, the resize
       stripes and the first-activation flash stayed WHITE - so the white sits IN FRONT of the
       compositor, and the only layer there is the framework page itself. Its VirtualHtml template
       ships html,body{height:100%} with NO background: a UA-default white body that stays white
       until the theme CSS arrives, with our dark boxes painted on top - white shows at first paint
       and wherever the boxes have not re-covered during a live resize.

       The override injects an INLINE background into the template, so it applies at the very first
       paint, before any external CSS - and permanently, so a resize-exposed body region is dark
       too. giFTThemeMode is seeded by ApplyFTTheme at construction; VirtualHtml runs at OnCreate,
       which is later. The Replaces target is the template's own spelling, "html, body{" - if a
       DataFlex update rewords the template the inject silently misses, so a future white flash
       means: read the base VirtualHtml again. */
    Function VirtualHtml Returns String
        String sHtml sBg sTheme sLinks sImportant

        Forward Get VirtualHtml to sHtml
        /* THE STYLESHEETS GO IN THE HEAD (Nils 2026-09-19: on the first open of the Guided board for
           a 2005-era workspace "the whole view area is shown with a black background and a lot of
           white text", gone before it can be read). The framework's template links NO stylesheet;
           its engine script inserts DfEngine/system.css, the theme's theme.css and
           CssStyle/application.css at init - AFTER the mounting page, which already carries the first
           tile's markup, has painted. With a findings list of thousands of rows that unstyled paint
           (theme ink on the inline dark body, every band and row as bare text) lasts long enough to
           see. Importing tiles.css from application.css did not touch it, because application.css is
           itself one of the late three.

           So the same three sheets, in the engine's own order, are linked here in the head: a head
           <link> is render-blocking, so the first paint is styled. The engine looks for existing
           links by href substring ("DfEngine/system.css", "CssThemes/" + "theme.css",
           "CssStyle/application.css") and ADOPTS them instead of adding its own, so nothing loads
           twice and its later theme switch still rewrites the theme link it found. */
        Get psTheme to sTheme
        If (Trim(sTheme) = "") ;
            Move "Df_Material" to sTheme
        Move ((StyleSheetLink("DfEngine/system.css")) ;
            + (StyleSheetLink("CssThemes/" + sTheme + "/theme.css")) ;
            + (StyleSheetLink("CssStyle/application.css")) ;
            + C_NewLine + '<!-- DataFlex Engine -->') to sLinks
        Move (Replaces("<!-- DataFlex Engine -->", sHtml, sLinks)) to sHtml
        // The web backdrop as CSS, from its Define (FTWebBg: clLightWebBg / clDarkWebBg). Character(35) is the
        // "#" - in a string literal a "#" before a letter can be read as a compiler directive.
        Move (Character(35) + FTColorHex(FTWebBg())) to sBg
        /* Probe 3 (green body, white band ABOVE it) placed the residual white exactly where
           application.css already documents it: the theme paints two opaque #EFEFEF layers ON TOP
           of #viewport - .WebApp.WebContainer and .WebCon_ContentWrp. application.css wins the
           steady state because it loads AFTER the theme, but that is precisely too late for the
           first-activation flash and the resize lag. So the same surfaces are painted here, inline
           in the head with !important - dark from the first style computation, before any sheet
           has loaded, and immune to the theme arriving later. */
        /* THE "!" IS ASSEMBLED, NEVER WRITTEN (2026-09-24). Inside a string literal the compiler reads
           "!i" as a macro token - the family of "!C" and "!1" - so " !important" compiled as
           " 0mportant", an invalid declaration, and the browser dropped both rules below. DFRefactor
           never showed it: its application.css paints the same surfaces again. The RDCFlexTron demo,
           which has no application.css, showed the theme's own #191919 behind every page.
           Character(33) is the "!".

           The three surfaces take var(--dfr-bg-page), with the theme colour as the fallback for the
           first paint, before palette.css has loaded. The token follows the class the framework puts
           on the body, so with "Follow Windows" the backdrop matches the web content drawn on it. */
        Move (Character(33) + "important") to sImportant
        Move (Replaces("html, body{", sHtml, "html, body{ background: " + sBg + " " + sImportant + ";")) to sHtml
        Move (Replaces(CS_StyleEnd, sHtml, "#viewport, .WebApp.WebContainer, .WebCon_ContentWrp { background: var(--dfr-bg-page, " + sBg + ") " + sImportant + "; } " + CS_StyleEnd)) to sHtml
        // The page says it is an FT host's (ApplyFTHostClass above): the body carries FtHost too. The target is
        // the template's own "<body>" - reworded by a DataFlex update, the body goes without, and the host's own
        // element still carries the class.
        Move (Replaces("<body>", sHtml, '<body class="FtHost">')) to sHtml
        Function_Return sHtml
    End_Function

    /* THE LIBRARY'S OWN CONTROLS COME WITH EVERY FT PAGE (2026-09-26). cFTWebButton's client class
       (ftcontrols.js), the FT look for real web controls (ftcontrols.css, with palette.css for its
       tokens), the button glyphs (ftglyphs.css, 2026-09-28) and an application's own (ftglyphs-app.css,
       2026-09-29, when it has any - after the library's, so one can redraw a library glyph) join the
       includes the framework writes into the host page. OnDefineScriptIncludes is
       the framework's own hook for that (cLocalWebControlHost.VirtualHtml): what it adds lands after
       FlexTron's own includes - df-min.js among them - so df.WebButton exists when the script runs,
       and after the theme, so the sheet wins over it. A page that uses none of it loads a few small
       files more. An object that augments this event must Forward it, or its page loses them. */
    Procedure OnDefineScriptIncludes String[] ByRef aScriptHtml
        Forward Send OnDefineScriptIncludes (&aScriptHtml)
        Move (StyleSheetLink("RDCFlexTron/palette.css")) to aScriptHtml[-1]
        Move (StyleSheetLink("RDCFlexTron/ftcontrols.css?v=" + CS_FTLibraryVersion)) to aScriptHtml[-1]
        Move (StyleSheetLink("RDCFlexTron/ftglyphs.css?v=" + CS_FTLibraryVersion)) to aScriptHtml[-1]
        If (SizeOfArray(FTAppGlyphNames()) > 0) ;
            Move (StyleSheetLink(CS_FTAppGlyphFolder + "/" + CS_FTAppGlyphSheet + "?v=" + FTAppGlyphStamp())) to aScriptHtml[-1]
        Move (ScriptLink("RDCFlexTron/ftcontrols.js?v=" + CS_FTLibraryVersion)) to aScriptHtml[-1]
    End_Procedure

    /*  A NAVIGATION MAKES THE PAGE UNREADY AGAIN, and the framework's own flag does not say so.

        pbInitWebApp is half of the readiness truth every live push is gated on (FTHostPageIsUp, in
        FTPageIsUp.pkg: the host is active, and this flag is False - cFTWebHtmlBox.UpdateHtml folds the
        push into Set psHtml otherwise). But the framework starts it True, sets it False ONCE
        in FinishWebObjectInitialize, and NEVER restores it. So it really means "has this host ever
        initialised", not "is the page ready now".

        That is the whole bug behind the message box's "Unhandled Program Error on the Client - Could
        not find object 'oMbBody_hb'" (Nils, seen repeatedly 2026-08-21, on the SECOND and later boxes
        of a session and reproducible through Uninstall, which is not our code). The framework's own
        OnNavigationStarting CLEARS the page-scoped data - the client's object registry - on every
        navigation, yet leaves pbInitWebApp False. The gate therefore lets a live ClientAction through to
        a page whose objects no longer exist, and the framework paints its error banner where the body
        should be. Intermittent, because it is a race with the navigation completing.

        Setting it back to True here restores the flag's intended meaning: from the moment a navigation
        starts until the page calls home again, pushes fold into Set psHtml - which is exactly the
        carrier a mounting page is served, and exactly why the FIRST box of a session never fails.

        SAFE WHEN NOTHING RENAVIGATES: this only fires when a navigation actually starts. A host whose
        page stays alive never reaches here, keeps pbInitWebApp False, and keeps taking the live path -
        so a box that is merely re-shown is not quietly served stale HTML.

        Forwarded, not replaced: the base clears the client and server handler data here, and losing that
        would leave stale web-property state behind. */
    Procedure OnNavigationStarting UBigInt iNavigationId String sUrl Boolean bIsRedirected ;
                                   Boolean bIsUserInitiated Boolean ByRef bCancelnavigation
        Set pbInitWebApp to True
        Forward Send OnNavigationStarting iNavigationId sUrl bIsRedirected bIsUserInitiated (&bCancelnavigation)
    End_Procedure

    /*  ...AND SO DOES A NEW CONTROL (0.9.24). A host is a control underneath, created each time its dialog
        or view is activated, and it navigates as it is created (the framework's OnCreate). The navigation's
        own event, above, comes a moment later through the message queue; code that runs straight after an
        activation - before that event - would still read the flag the page before left behind. So the flag
        is set here already, at the moment the new page is asked for, as it is on the very first showing.
        With the host's Active_State it makes FTHostPageIsUp true to its name: a page is up while its host
        is active and the page has called home, and at no other time. */
    Procedure OnCreate
        Set pbInitWebApp to True
        Forward Send OnCreate
    End_Procedure

End_Class
